This keeps the recursion simple and lets `set<vector<int>>` remove repeated results. The input is sorted first, so every subset is stored in the same value order. It is easier to write, but it uses more memory and has slower set operations than skipping duplicates while searching.

The dry run for `[1,2,2]` creates the empty subset and all include or exclude paths. Some paths create the same vector, but the set stores that vector only once.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    void generate(int index, const vector<int>& nums,
                  vector<int>& current, set<vector<int>>& unique) {
        if (index == static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            unique.insert(current);
            return;
        }

        generate(index + 1, nums, current, unique);

        current.push_back(nums[index]);
        generate(index + 1, nums, current, unique);
        current.pop_back();
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int> > subsetsWithDup(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        set<vector<int>> unique;
        vector<int> current;
        generate(0, nums, current, unique);

        return vector<vector<int>>(unique.begin(), unique.end());
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n * log(2^n))` as a rough bound for generating, copying, and inserting subsets into the set.

Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the set and the returned vector, plus `O(n)` recursion space.

Sorting puts equal values next to each other. During backtracking, the first equal value at a level is allowed, but later equal values at that same level are skipped. A duplicate can still be chosen deeper in the tree, so inputs like `[2,2]` still produce `[2,2]`.

The input vector is sorted in place.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    void build(int start, vector<int>& nums, vector<int>& current,
               vector<vector<int>>& result) {
        result.push_back(current);

        for (int i = start; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                continue;
            }

            current.push_back(nums[i]);
            build(i + 1, nums, current, result);
            current.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int> > subsetsWithDup(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        vector<vector<int>> result;
        vector<int> current;
        build(0, nums, current, result);
        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` in the worst case because each saved subset may take `O(n)` time to copy.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current subset and recursion stack, not counting the returned subsets.

This version builds the answer one value group at a time. If a value appears three times, every old subset gets one copy, two copies, and three copies of that value. This directly matches the choices available for repeated values.

For `[1,2,2]`, the empty subset first becomes `[[], [1]]`. The two `2` values then add `[2]`, `[1,2]`, `[2,2]`, and `[1,2,2]`.

The array is sorted in place, but the result lists are newly created.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        result.add(new ArrayList<>());

        int index = 0;
        while (index < nums.length) {
            int end = index;
            while (end < nums.length && nums[end] == nums[index]) {
                end++;
            }

            int oldSize = result.size();
            int count = end - index;

            for (int i = 0; i < oldSize; i++) {
                List<Integer> base = result.get(i);
                List<Integer> next = new ArrayList<>(base);

                for (int copies = 1; copies <= count; copies++) {
                    next.add(nums[index]);
                    result.add(new ArrayList<>(next));
                }
            }

            index = end;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` when counting the cost of copying subsets.

Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the returned list of subsets. The working list uses less space than the output.

